I look at it this way.  One may communicate with McElwere via email; granted,
he doesn't seem to answer it, but it *is* available.  When one decides to start
mailbombing his sysadmin, one has begun to abuse the facilities.  

If one of my users were mailbombing McElwere *or* his sysadmin, I'd have
a chat with him.  It may not be a 'major violation', but it is both im-
proper and harassing. (Of course, I'd also check my mailer logs to be
sure that there really *was* mailbombing, not just one 'put a lid on
this guy' message.)

Remember, Usenet is not equivalent to email; there are different rules for
each forum.  McElwere's postings, while painfully omnipresent, don't impede
your work; your mailbombs to his sysadmin, on the other hand, most certainly
do.  There's a difference.

Remember, too, that sysadmins shouldn't be expected to act as net.enforcers.
If you find something illegal or *truly* improper (such as posting credit
card numbers, cracked passwords, student transcripts and the like), it's
entirely proper to involve the sysadmin (in my opinion).  If you just don't
like what he's saying, don't expect the sysadmin to take action for you.

I suggest that you use a killfile, if your newsreaders supports them; it's
done wonders for me.  If you can't use a killfile, learn to hit the 'n'
key.  You could even write your own pseudo-killfile; just look thorugh the
news articles for his address and mark them as read in your newsreader's
update file.

Quite frankly, it sounds like you don't like the goose's sauce.

