------------------------------------------------------------------------------
		       The Ohio State University
	      Computer and Information Sciences Department
			Policies and Guidelines

1 The Philosophy Behind the Policies

The department's computer facilities are limited. We generally rely on
our users to keep in mind that there are other users, and to be
considerate of those other users. Most of these policies simply expand
on this concept. The policies and guidelines detailed in this document
are not an exhaustive list of what users should and should not do. They
describe specific behaviors which are or are not acceptable, to varying
degrees. If a problem comes up that is not explicitly covered by the
policies and guidelines, look to the existing policies and guidelines,
and follow the spirit of those rules.

2 Official CIS Department Policies (short form)

These are actual departmental policies. Violation of departmental
policies can lead to various penalties. Severe and/or repeated
violations can lead to anything from loss of privileges on your account
to a charge of academic misconduct. Academic misconduct charges will be
handled by the department according to the University's standard
procedures.

* Users doing CIS course work have top priority on the CIS workstations
in the labs.

* Do not give anyone else access to your account.

* Use machines set aside for remote use when you are logged in remotely.

* Do not tamper with someone else's workstation, processes, or jobs.

* Printer use should be kept to a minimum. Printer use is audited, and
users who seem to print far more than the average may be asked to
account for their use of the facilities.

* If you need multiple copies of a printout, print it once and use a copier.

* The contents of each user's files are that user's personal property.
No one should read or copy another user's files without that user's
permission.

* Stay within your disk quota. If you fail to do so, your account may be 
deactivated.

* Do not try to break system security for any reason.

* You must log out from your workstation if you will be gone from it for
more than 15 minutes.

* While game playing is allowed on CIS systems, it is restricted to
times when fewer than one-half of the workstations in a lab are
occupied.

2.1 Rules of Etiquette (short form)

Below are some basic rules of etiquette that CIS-IICF users are expected
to follow.

* Check files before you print them out to make sure their contents are
what you expect.

* Limit yourself to a maximum of five print jobs on the printer queue at
any given time.

* Learn to use mail and news.

* Read cis.general and your mail to avoid missing important notices.

* Change your password at least once a quarter.

* Use the basic UNIX file protection scheme to prevent others from
reading or copying your personal files, including lab programs.

* Be aware of scams people may run to gain access to your account.

* In general, treat your fellow local and global computer users in a
polite and civilized manner.

3 Official Policies and Rules of Etiquette Explained

Appropriate Use

As a user of a CIS computer account, you are a part of an electronic
community. As with conventional communities, everything runs more
smoothly when everyone cooperates. You are expected to use your account
in an appropriate fashion. Appropriate use is an ill-defined term. a
number of specifics about what is and is not considered to be
appropriate use are detailed in this document. However, this list cannot
be comprehensive. In general, we request that our users use simple
common sense when using their accounts, and try to be polite to their
fellow users. If you try to follow this philosophy, you are unlikely to
run afoul of the department's policies.

Priority Use of CIS Computing Facilities

You may use your CIS account not only for CIS lab work, but for other
CIS course work, for any course work in general, and even for
recreation. However, since these facilities are provided by the CIS
department, users working on CIS course work have top priority. In
addition, If you log into a CIS computer remotely, you are expected to
use one of the machines reserved explicitly for remote users. 
category they fall into. The current disk quotas are: 

    Undergraduates--1 megabyte(MB)
    Guests--1 MB
    Graduate Students--1.5 MB
    CIS Staff--2.5 MB
    CIS Faculty--2.5 MB

Users who are taking more than one CIS course, or certain CIS courses
that require extremely large files, will have their disk quotas
increased. Quotas are increased by .5 MB for each extra course. Users
taking graphics courses get an extra 1 MB, since the lab projects they
will be working on tend to result in large files. For similar reasons,
users taking operating systems courses with lab projects get an extra
1.5 MB. The increased quota will return to the standard default at the
end of the quarter.

Appropriate Use of CIS Disk Space

At present, the disk quotas are not rigidly enforced. In general, the
user community has made reasonable use of disk space, so strict
enforcement of the quotas has not been necessary. However, disk usage is
regularly monitored. To find out how much disk space you are using, use
the du -s command in your home directory. Users who are significantly
over quota are notified by electronic mail. Users who are shown to be
significantly over quota in two consecutive audits are brought to the
attention of the chair of the CIS Computer Committee and the manager of
the IICF. Those users will be asked to explain why they have not
complied with the requests to reduce their disk usage. Users who do not
comply and who cannot adequately explain their reasons may lose their
computer privileges until the situation is resolved.

In emergency situations, the CIS systems and operations staff may delete
file from user accounts in order to get a file server back into service.
The files are found by an automated clean-up program, which looks for
such things as out-of-date Emacs backup files, and files that can be
easily replaced. If the clean-up process does not free up enough disk
space, the accounts of users who are severely over quota may be looked
at, in an attempt to locate files that are obviously not CIS-related.
Note that this in only done if a clean-up does not release enough disk
space for the file server to be usable.

Appropriate Use of Network Resources

The ability to communicate with other users, from those in the
department to people on the other side of the world, can be an
invaluable tool. You are expected to learn how to read electronic mail
and news. In particular, you are expected to keep up with the
cis.general newsgroup. Important information, such as computer downtime
and changes to various departmental procedures, are announced
there.Further, the ability to access other systems to run programs or
download source files is also quite useful. Our users are expected to be
reasonable and polite in their use of these facilities. They are
expected to know and follow both the mail and news etiquette guidelines
listed in Chapter 5, and the rules of the various organizations that
operate the computer networks to which we have access. The most notable
of those guidelines are:

* Illegal activities are prohibited. Including, but not limited to,
slander, libel, or harassment.

* Obscene language and personal attacks are strictly prohibited.

* Activities which interfere with the operation of a network are
prohibited, including the propagation of computer worms, viruses trojan
horses.

* As noted elsewhere, violations of the security systems are prohibited.

* Attempts to send mail or post news with forged headers, so that they
appear to be from someone else.

* Mailing or posting chain letters is prohibited.

* Unsolicited messages from a commercial enterprise, advertising its own
goods and/or services, are generally inappropriate.

* Postings of very large news articles should be limited to newsgroups
expressly designed for such postings, such as the comp.binaries groups.

* ".signature" files at the end of email and rn messages should be no
longer than four lines.

Complaints that a user has committed one of these offenses can lead to
removal of that user's mail and news privileges, other restrictions on
that user's account, and/or formal action by the department or
university including dismissal.

In general, if any CIS resource is used in the process of violating
University policy, it is considered abuse of your CIS privileges. For
example, if you have a background image on your machine that can be
considered sexually or racially insensitive or at worst, sexual or
racial harassment, you may be subject to discipline under both
University and CIS guidelines.

Security Violations

On occasion, people have tried to break into the department's systems by
figuring out the password to an account here. This is one of the main
reasons why you should use passwords that are difficult to guess, and
why you should changed passwords on a reasonable frequent basis. When
such security violations are discovered, the CIS systems and operations
staff may need to prevent this security breach from causing problems
here, and to assist outside authorities in their attempts to track down
the perpetrators. It may also be necessary to try to locate any trap
doors the perpetrators have added to the system, to make it easier for
them to get back in. Under these circumstances, the CIS staff will do
whatever is necessary to resolve the breach. This might include locking
an account which has been broken into. If at all possible, affected
users will be contacted in advance in order to inform them of the
situation. The staff may also request permission to examine users' files
for signs of tampering.

Local users found to be violating or attempting to violate the security
of the systems may face academic misconduct charges, or other
appropriate legal action. Using the department's systems to attempt to
violate the security of any other system will result in similar
consequences.

If you are concerned that there may be a security hole in the system,
bring your concerns to the systems staff. Do not, under any
circumstances, attempt to find a security hole in the system without
permission from the systems staff. The systems staff will treat any
attempt to violate security as malicious. Curiosity can not be used as
an excuse.

Locking Terminals and Workstations

The programs lock and xlock are provided so that users can lock their
terminals or workstations when they have to leave them briefly.
Terminals and workstations should be locked for no more than 15 minutes.
The locking programs are intended to allow users to safely leave their
machines long enough to go to the bathroom or stretch their legs. The
CIS operations staff will log users out of machines that have been
locked for more than 15 minutes, if other users are waiting for a free
machine.

Background Images

Computers in public labs and offices are the property of the University
and are part of the workplace. Therefore, images displayed on them
should be governed by principles concerning appropriate use of
University resources and a supportive, friendly work environment.

Many users like to display an image on the background of their machine
instead of the default gray background. There are publicly available
images in directory /usr/local/bitmap/raster. Other images may be
displayed if they meet the above guidelines and they can be stored
within the user's disk quota.

Any user who finds the display of a particular bitmap inappropriate may
notify the operations staff. The staff will notify the displayer to
remove the bitmap immediately and to refrain from further public
display. The bitmap will be removed from the CIS public directory if it
resides there.

Game Playing

Games are available on the CIS computer systems. However, users should
not use a workstation exclusively for game playing when the lab they are
in is more than half full. CIS operators have the authority to ask users
who are playing games under those circumstances to stop doing so, and to
surrender their workstation if they have no course work to do.

4 Questions Regarding CIS Policies

If you have any questions regarding the CIS department policies, please
contact the chair of the CIS Computer Committee.

