Notes 1-4 concern logging anonymous ftp usage.

1. Many anonymous ftp servers log host name, host address, identity,
password, and file transfers for anonymous users, with no stated
policy.  Is this proper and should the logs be public record, or is
this an invasion of privacy?
    <1993Feb3.103442.12179@nntp.hut.fi>

2. "The main reason system administrators log anonymous ftp
activity... is for system security."  Ftp is an attack point of
crackers; logs can help identify crackers, security holes, and
provide information for possible prosecution.
    <1993Feb3.211057.22700@netcom.com>

3. "Ftp logs here are definitely NOT considered public records...
naive users of anonymous FTP might actually type in their password."
    <1993Feb3.214249.9068@mp.cs.niu.edu>

4. "A site should make its policy on log information known to the user
in advance."  If you don't see an explicit statement of policy, don't
presume one.
    <1ks213$in6@nic.near.net>

Notes 5-6 concern file privacy

5. My account was terminated after the files on my account were
examined by the system administrator.
    <38470@uflorida.cis.ufl.edu>

6. A search may have violated your 4th Amendment right against
unreasonable search, and the termination of your account may have
violated your constitutional and contractual right to due process.
Punishment may violate your rights to freedom of expression.
    <1993Feb3.225041.10325@eff.org>

Notes 7-8 concern the rights of an expired account

7. An account that was supposed to have been terminated appears to
have been used for mischief such as password cracking.  What action
may we take against the owner, who is no longer a student?
    <1993Feb4.013614.25163@scott.skidmore.edu>

8. If it can be shown that the student knew it was not OK to continue
using the account, then pursue the matter to the fullest.
    <3FEB199323193511@ctrvx1.vanderbilt.edu>

Note 9-10 concern the ethics of virus distribution

9. Is it a good idea to distribute computer viruses?
    <1993Feb4.101803.29931@nntp.hut.fi>

10. "Distribution of ANY program, regardless of its intended purpose
or use should NEVER be made illegal."
    <93036.140046JDG111@psuvm.psu.edu>

Note 11 describe problems with ftp at SRI

11. I was searching for AI code at SRI available via anonymous FTP, and
looked at their password file (I didn't know this at the time).  It
turns out that the administrator at SRI had called my school's
computer services, which in turn called my department chair, who
called my professor, who warned me not to try to break into SRI's
computer system.
    <1993Feb4.230239.5445@memstvx1.memst.edu>

