I thought that you all might be interested to hear this:

Here at Penn State University we have an independent publication called the
Lionhearted.  This publication is definitely oriented towards a perspective
from the right-wing and they often publish articles which condemn women,
minorities, and gays, lesbians, and bisexuals.  In protest, a lecturer in
the Women Studies department is urging people to steal copies of this paper
(which, incidentially, is free) and dispose of this 'hate speech'.
Mysteriously 6,000 copies of the paper disappear and about half of them are
burnt on the lawn of the man who funds this paper (and he happens to be on
the board of trustees).

Granted, I am a minority and I find the Lionhearted completely offensive.
Yet I cannot condone people limiting the free speech of others by stealing
the papers.  I am curious to hear what everyone thinks about a) the paper
being 'stolen' even though it is free b) the fact that a member of the board
of trustees supports this publication.  I welcome any discussion on this
topic.
