Seems reasonable. I believe Ohio State U's Judicial Committee, not its
academic violates committee, has jurisdiction on most types of
student computer infractions.

[...]
>Displaying offensive bitmaps in a public lab is not allowed. A
>conservative interpretation of 'offensive' should be used.

Offensive material is generally Constitutionally protected.  This
policy could be improved by giving or summarizing the University's
general harassment policy and procedure.

>Notice that copying (i.e., stealing or "pirating") computer software
>is also prohibited under this policy.

What about software with a copyright and/or license that allows
copying?

>3.8 Electronic Mail
[...]
>communication (see short subject documents available in HI 314).  All
>messages sent anywhere by any one must be "appropriate" (see short
>subject document on "Standards and Customs").

[I've asked Dr. Parent for a copy of this document.]

>While the Department has no desire to try to censor electronic
>messages, it does have a specific obligation to the organizations that
>operate computer networks to which the Department connects. In
>addition, the University has guidelines for acceptable behavior on the
>network. The Department abides by these guidelines and adopts them as
>defining acceptable behavior on its network. Without exception, these
>rules prohibit obscene language, graphics, personal attacks, attempts
>to send anonymous messages, using too much bandwidth, participation in
>or promoting chain messages and a variety of other unsociable acts.

Vulgar language is Constitutionally protected. 'In Papish v.
University of Missouri, 410 U.S.  667, 93 S.Ct. 1197, 35 L.Ed.2d 618
(1973), the Supreme Court ordered the reinstatement of a university
student expelled for distributing an underground newspaper sporting
the headline "Motherfucker acquitted" on the grounds that "the mere
dissemination of ideas--no matter how offensive to good taste--on a
state university campus may not be shut off in the name alone of
conventions of decency."' [Quote from _Doe v. U. of Michigan.]

So are most sexually-explicit images and most verbal attacks in public
forums (see refs). It is very unlikely that the University prohibits
these.

Finally, does the University ban anonymous expression? In _Tally v.
California_ the Surpreme Court said: "{a}nonymously pamphlets,
leaflets, brochures and even books have played an important role in
the progress of mankind. Persecuted groups and sects from time to time
throughout history have been able to criticize oppressive practices
and laws either anonymously or not at all."

>4 Bitmaps

>Any user who finds the display of a particular bitmap inappropriate
>may notify the operations staff. The staff will notify the displayer
>to remove the bitmap immediately and to refrain from further public
>display. The bitmap will be removed from the CIS public directory if
>it resides there.

As mentioned before, this rule is likely contracts University sexual
harassment policy and First Amendment protections.

- Carl Kadie

