From caf-talk Caf Apr 29 20:58:50 1993
Newsgroups: comp.admin.policy,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,cis.opinion
From: kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: Newer Policy from Ohio State University's CIS Dept.
Message-ID: <1993Apr30.005841.10262@eff.org>
Date: Fri, 30 Apr 1993 00:58:41 GMT

Policies of the CIS Department says:

>[T]he Department ... does have a specific obligation to the
>organizations that operate computer networks to which the Department
>connects. In addition, the University has guidelines for acceptable
>behavior on the network. ... Without exception, these rules prohibit
>obscene language, graphics, personal attacks, attempts to send
>anonymous messages, using too much bandwidth, participation in or
>promoting chain messages and a variety of other unsociable acts.

J. Greely (jgreely@morganucodon.cis.ohio-state.edu) sent me pointers
to the other AUP's. Here is what they say:

OARnet says: "It is not acceptable to use OARnet to transmit
threatening, obscene, or harassing materials."

CICNet doesn't have any such detailed prohibitions.

ANS says: "ANS networks must not be used to transmit any communication
where the meaning of the message, or its transmission or distribution,
would violate any applicable law or regulation or would likely be
highly offensive to the recipient or recipients thereof."

The CIS policy says "without exception", but I see nothing but
exceptions. None of the network policies (as I understand them)
prohibit all vulgar language, nonobscene graphics, nonharassing
personal attacks (say in a open forum), or attempts to send anoymous
messages.

The ANS policy could be read as prohibiting the sometimes highly
offensive rec.humor.funny newsgroup, but I think it more likely that
the policy prohibits only highly offensive *unsolicited*
communications (in other words, harassment).

- Carl
