In article <1993Apr30.005841.10262@eff.org>, and quite a few others,
 kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes all sorts of things, all the time.

Y'know, KCarl, It occurs to me that the simplest way to revise our
bitmap policy in accord with your statements is to flatly prohibit
display of background images in our public labs.  No one's rights
would be violated, our policy would be more consistent, and we could
quit wasting everyone's time on this nonsense and get on with our
lives (well, some of us could).

  In fact, we could go further in accord with your wishes, by
reviewing all system uses that don't fit into our funded purpose, and
removing them.  Usenet would go (except perhaps for comp.* and local
groups), games would go, most of our ftp and uucp archives would be
history, etc.

  Think of the money we'd save, and how much better we'd be able to
service (er... "serve") our users!  They wouldn't have been highly
inconvenienced by a user with delusions of adequacy yesterday (many
losing a dozen or more e-mail messages), and I wouldn't have had to
spend hours of my reasonably-expensive time cleaning up after his
abuse.  It's clear that we give our users far too much rope here, and
the tighter we tie them down, the better off they'll all be.  You
won't have any reason to complain about our policy documents, because
academic freedom won't be an issue when the system is forcibly
restricted to supporting computer science classwork.

  Am I kidding?  Not really.  It almost happened once, after a student
expressed himself by putting sexually explicit images on someone
else's screen.  We *could* (legally, ethically, and practically) be
quite strict about how our resources are used; we're not.  We give our
users as much freedom as we reasonably can, certainly more than enough
to get their heads lopped off.  If they behave in even vaguely
rational ways, we have no cause for complaint; out of 2500 users,
there are usually only a dozen or so that are obviously up to
something at any given time.  Mostly they get away with whatever it is
until they disrupt things enough that we spot them.

  A good rule of thumb around here is that if you can mention
someone's name to the staff and get more than half to say, "Oh, God,
what's he up to now", there's a problem.  If most of us know who you
are, you're almost certainly either really good, really loud, and/or a
real bozo (or faculty, I suppose, although some might argue that
they're already covered by those three categories :-)).  Yes, I *am*
saying that "round up the usual suspects" is on my checklist for
resolving system problems.


